sentences.l:
and after him rose Atreus’-son, fair-haired Menelaus,
Zeus-sprung, and under the yoke he led swift horses,
Aethe, Agamemnon’s mare, and his own Podargus;

Her to Agamemnon gave Anchises’-son Echepolus
as a gift, so that he not follow him to windy Ilios,
but right there take his pleasure, remaining;

And Antilochus fourth made ready well-maned horses,
the splendid son of Nestor, the high-spirited lord,
the Neleus’-son;

“Antilochus, indeed you, though being young, they loved,
Zeus and Poseidon, and horsemanship they taught you
of every kind;

But the man who, trusting in his own horses and chariot,
senselessly wheels over a wide space this way and that,
and his horses wander along the course, and he does not hold them;

To it you, pressing in very close, drive near chariot and horses,
and yourself lean in the well-woven chariot
gently to the left of the two;

And at the turning-point let the left-hand horse be pressed close,
so that to you the nave will seem to reach the edge
of the well-made wheel;

And they all together over the two horses raised the whips,
and struck with straps, and shouted at them with words
eagerly;

and the drivers
stood in the chariots, and the heart of each pounded,
of them striving for victory;
